# 17. UI/UX Design System

## 17.1 Brand Colors

The primary brand color is Orangered.

| Color | Hex Code | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| Orangered | `#FF4500` | Primary buttons, active states, conversation accents |
| Black | `#000000` | Main text and structural elements |
| White | `#FFFFFF` | Backgrounds and cards |
| Light Gray | `#F6F6F6` | Secondary backgrounds |
| Border Gray | `#E5E5E5` | Card borders and dividers |
| Secondary Text | `#666666` | Metadata and descriptions |
| Success Green | `#21865B` | Resolved status |
| Error Red | `#C62828` | Error messages and moderation warnings |

Orangered should be the recognizable signature color.

Use it strategically rather than making every component bright orange.

## 17.2 Typography

Use a clean, readable sans-serif font such as Inter.

Maintain a consistent hierarchy:
- Page headings: 24–32px.
- Post titles: 17–20px.
- Body text: 14–16px.
- Metadata: 12–13px.

## 17.3 Visual Style

- White cards.
- Light neutral backgrounds.
- Thin borders.
- Moderate rounded corners.
- Clear spacing.
- Minimal shadows.
- Simple line icons.
- Consistent hover and focus states.
- Subtle, short transitions.

Avoid unnecessary gradients, excessive animations, and complicated visual effects.

## 17.4 Mobile-First Design

The website must work comfortably on common Android smartphones.

On mobile:
- Use a single-column feed.
- Provide compact top navigation.
- Use a bottom navigation bar.
- Make Create easy to reach.
- Keep touch targets comfortable.
- Prevent horizontal overflow.
- Respect safe areas.
- Ensure the keyboard does not obscure the post form.

On desktop:
- Use a centered content layout.
- Add a compact left navigation sidebar if useful.
- Keep the feed approximately 600–700px wide.
- Use the remaining space for useful discovery components.

